{"agent":"impl-sw-conformance","data":{"by":"Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"The knob is checked with `_count`, which permits `0`. If an operator sets `swarm.conformance_min_calls = 0`, then a conformance run that observes zero tool calls is not “fewer” than the minimum, so the documented “fewer is `unavailable`, never passed” gate is disabled and the run proceeds to judge accuracy on zero calls. That either makes the accuracy computation invalid (division by zero if not guarded) or allows an empty run to be treated as available/passed, contradicting the intent that “unavailable is not passed.” The minimum call count should be validated as positive, or the zero-call case must be forced unavailable before accuracy is judged.","digest":"94f61dadea2900e0","location":"ddflow/config_sections/swarm.py (`conformance_min_calls`)","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/config_sections/swarm.py (`conformance_min_calls`)"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"`grade` treats launch as passed unless `stopped_clean` is explicitly `False`. It passes when `stopped_clean` is `None`, and `observe` sets `stopped_clean = driver.stop(h).status != \"failed\"`, so a stop result with status `\"unavailable\"` is also treated as clean. For example, `grade(\"lane\", Observed(started=True), Params())` returns `launch` passed and `level=1` even though no clean stop was observed. In a live run, if `driver.stop(h)` returns `Result(status=\"unavailable\", ...)`, the lane is graded as having stopped cleanly, contrary to the stated rule that unavailable is not passed and launch requires a clean stop.","digest":"975dd5ac4d5f6930","location":"ddflow/services/swarm/conformance.py:grade/observe","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/swarm/conformance.py:grade/observe"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"`admits` converts a string `needed` level with `LEVEL_NAMES.index(needed)`, so an unknown level name raises `ValueError` instead of denying admission. For example, `admits(Report(\"lane\", 5, (), 0.0), \"hook_fire\", Params())` crashes with `ValueError: 'hook_fire' is not in tuple`. A scheduler or config typo such as `\"hook_fire\"` for `\"hook\"` therefore takes down the admission check rather than returning `False`.","digest":"55278752a9d4e885","location":"ddflow/services/swarm/conformance.py:admits","severity":"LOW","title":"ddflow/services/swarm/conformance.py:admits"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"`norm_tool` strips prefixes only in the order they appear in `_PREFIXES`, so it fails to strip a generic prefix after `ddflow.` or a repeated `ddflow_` prefix. For example, `norm_tool(\"ddflow.ddflow_claim\")` returns `ddflow_claim`, not `claim`, and `norm_tool(\"ddflow_ddflow_claim\")` also returns `ddflow_claim`. A stub endpoint or tool event using such a name will not match `_called(..., \"claim\")` or normalized `Params.tools` entries for `claim`, so the claim check or tool accuracy can fail even though the lane called the claim tool.","digest":"dda223ce768ad61e","location":"ddflow/services/swarm/conformance.py:norm_tool","severity":"LOW","title":"ddflow/services/swarm/conformance.py:norm_tool"},{"chunk":3,"detail":"`save()` is not safe for concurrent updates even though the final `os.replace()` is atomic. It performs a non-atomic read-modify-write: `load_all()` reads the current lane set, then the new lane is added and the whole file is replaced. If two processes (or threads in one process) call `save()` for different lanes, both can read the same prior state before either writes. For example, starting from no lane levels, process A calls `save(lane_a)`, process B calls `save(lane_b)`, and the interleaving is A load, B load, A replace, B replace. The final file contains only `lane_b`, silently erasing `lane_a`’s conformance level even though it passed. This can leave a lane unadmitted despite a recorded pass.","digest":"75c92e22d83e6cc7","location":"ddflow/services/swarm/conformance_io.py:40","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/swarm/conformance_io.py:40"},{"chunk":3,"detail":"`load_all()` treats any schema other than `lane-levels@1` as if there are no lanes, and `save()` then uses that empty result to overwrite the file with schema `lane-levels@1`. If the file is written by a newer version, e.g. `.ddflow/local/lane-levels.json` contains `{\"schema\": \"lane-levels@2\", \"lanes\": {\"lane-a\": ...}}`, then `load_all()` returns `{}`. A subsequent `save(report)` for `lane-b` writes a new file containing only `lane-b` under `lane-levels@1`, deleting all other lanes and downgrading the schema. An unknown/new schema should be rejected or preserved, not silently clobbered.","digest":"e7017b823eea0e99","location":"ddflow/services/swarm/conformance_io.py:27","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/swarm/conformance_io.py:27"},{"chunk":5,"detail":"The test is named for “a gate call for another action,” but it never supplies a gate call. `grade_calls([\"claim\", \"heartbeat\"], action=\"list\")` produces only `claim` and `heartbeat` events, so `gate_record == FAILED` is true simply because no gate event exists. If `conformance.grade` ignored the `action` field and treated any `gate` call as a record, this test would still pass; it does not actually refute the behavior it claims to test.","digest":"4179fb649674e39f","location":"tests/test_lane_conformance.py:test_gate_call_for_another_action_is_not_a_record","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"tests/test_lane_conformance.py:test_gate_call_for_another_action_is_not_a_record"},{"chunk":5,"detail":"The helper used by the accuracy tests does not construct a valid gate-record call, yet the tests assert a level beyond gate-record. `grade_calls(base + [\"bash\"] * 2)` and `grade_calls([\"claim\", \"heartbeat\", \"gate\"] + [\"bash\"] * 2)` include a call named `gate` but omit `action=\"record\"`, while asserting `level == 6`, i.e. after gate-record/hook/lease-stop have passed. If gate-record requires `action=\"record\"` (as the `GOOD` script and the other test’s name imply), those reports cannot legitimately reach level 6. If they do, the test suite is certifying that gate-record ignores the action, contradicting the gate-record requirement.","digest":"67433583b84b1148","location":"tests/test_lane_conformance.py:grade_calls","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"tests/test_lane_conformance.py:grade_calls"}],"chunks_total":5,"coverage":"5/5 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":30438,"diff_sha":"20f42075fa2885a6b94fc3c7d5f7e4d7e0a0ffe172ba78c58de9a04f894397f9","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in [REDACTED:path]","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":275.5,"excluded_bytes":4853,"excluded_files":2,"excluded_paths":["README.md","tests/golden/__snapshots__/test_golden_outputs.ambr"],"family":"alibaba","findings":8,"max_chunk_chars":5000,"model":"Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8","output_digest":"ed740cba11349b56","output_file":".ddflow/local/reviews/SW-conformance.rubber_duck.qwen3.8-flash-next-fp8.20261030T184002Z-1506daff.jsonl","reason":"","review_kind":"full","reviewed":[1,2,3,4,5],"reviewed_head":"f8834bad499c5166bbd8a7f9bfb9254e5688d74a","reviewed_paths":{"README.md":"100644 2c32cb3db2d13f1a9d2b53f2b0c1e736a2ebebcc","ddflow/config_sections/swarm.py":"100644 62b6e87012278deb484337e28c58275c253b2625","ddflow/services/swarm/conformance.py":"100644 aaf58d359ad86be08b35883e77a6ad5881021d5c","ddflow/services/swarm/conformance_io.py":"100644 a00d45b108bc6cb7d5a9c5efac477952aef02fcf","ddflow/templates/upgrade/unreleased/knob_added.swarm.conformance_idle_s.toml":"100644 9a01ec4c1284829b7dcef148afd887ea57deb576","ddflow/templates/upgrade/unreleased/knob_added.swarm.conformance_lease_wait_s.toml":"100644 98c12f06b87992f6e16fb3785d2b0f7d1c93deb3","ddflow/templates/upgrade/unreleased/knob_added.swarm.conformance_max_age_s.toml":"100644 7437645ac8b4a36e19547c39f94cec5b5814d082","ddflow/templates/upgrade/unreleased/knob_added.swarm.conformance_min_accuracy.toml":"100644 26035d37f9ddcf